All work in this lab is performed inside your assigned, isolated practice environment. No production systems or external targets are involved.
Task 1: Deploy AWX and Configure Core Inventory
Subtask 1.1: Deploy AWX Using the AWX Operator Compose Method

AWX is the open-source project from which Red Hat Ansible Automation Platform's controller component is derived. We deploy it using the community-supported awx-on-docker compose approach so the whole stack runs in containers on a single controller node.
Step 1: Clone the deployment repository

git clone https://github.com/kurokobo/awx-on-docker.git
cd awx-on-docker

Step 2: Configure environment variables

cat > .env << 'EOF'
AWX_ADMIN_USER=admin
AWX_ADMIN_PASSWORD=Password123!
AWX_POSTGRES_PASSWORD=awxpostgrespass
AWX_HOST_PORT=8080
EOF

Step 3: Start the AWX stack

docker compose up -d
sleep 60
docker compose ps

You should see containers for awx-web, awx-task, and postgres all reporting Up (running) status.
Step 4: Access the AWX web interface

Open a browser and navigate to http://<controller-ip>:8080. Log in with:

    Username: admin
    Password: Password123!

Deliverable: A screenshot or terminal output of docker compose ps showing all three services running, plus a successful login to the AWX dashboard.
Subtask 1.2: Create the Production Inventory File
Step 1: Create the inventory directory and file

mkdir -p /opt/ansible-inventories
cat > /opt/ansible-inventories/production.yml << 'EOF'
all:
  children:
    webservers:
      hosts:
        web-server-1:
          ansible_host: 192.168.1.10
          ansible_user: ansible
          server_role: primary
      vars:
        http_port: 80
    databases:
      hosts:
        database-server:
          ansible_host: 192.168.1.20
          ansible_user: ansible
          db_type: postgresql
      vars:
        db_port: 5432
EOF

Step 2: Validate the inventory syntax

ansible-inventory -i /opt/ansible-inventories/production.yml --list

The output should be valid JSON containing webservers, databases, and a _meta.hostvars section listing both hosts with the variables you defined.
Step 3: Test connectivity to managed nodes

ansible -i /opt/ansible-inventories/production.yml all -m ping

Expected result: web-server-1 and database-server both return "ping": "pong" with a SUCCESS status. If SSH keys are not yet distributed, complete Task 3, Subtask 3.1 first, then re-run this command.

Deliverable: Successful JSON output from ansible-inventory --list and two SUCCESS responses from ansible ... -m ping.
Task 2: Build Playbooks and Configure AWX Job Templates
Subtask 2.1: Create the Web Server Deployment Playbook
Step 1: Create the playbook directory structure

mkdir -p /opt/ansible-playbooks/templates

Step 2: Write the web server playbook

cat > /opt/ansible-playbooks/deploy-webserver.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Deploy Web Server Infrastructure
  hosts: webservers
  become: yes
  vars:
    web_packages:
      - httpd
    web_service: httpd

  tasks:
    - name: Install web server packages
      dnf:
        name: "{{ web_packages }}"
        state: present

    - name: Deploy web application index page
      template:
        src: index.html.j2
        dest: /var/www/html/index.html
        owner: apache
        group: apache
        mode: '0644'
      notify: restart httpd

    - name: Start and enable web service
      systemd:
        name: "{{ web_service }}"
        state: started
        enabled: yes

    - name: Open firewall for HTTP
      firewalld:
        service: http
        permanent: yes
        state: enabled
        immediate: yes
      when: ansible_facts.services['firewalld.service'] is defined

  handlers:
    - name: restart httpd
      systemd:
        name: httpd
        state: restarted
EOF

Step 3: Create the index page template

cat > /opt/ansible-playbooks/templates/index.html.j2 << 'EOF'
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>{{ inventory_hostname }} - Lab Server</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Welcome to {{ inventory_hostname }}</h1>
    <p>Server Role: {{ server_role | default('undefined') }}</p>
    <p>Managed by: Ansible Automation Platform</p>
</body>
</html>
EOF

Deliverable: Running ansible-playbook -i /opt/ansible-inventories/production.yml /opt/ansible-playbooks/deploy-webserver.yml --syntax-check returns playbook: /opt/ansible-playbooks/deploy-webserver.yml with no errors.
Subtask 2.2: Create the Database Deployment Playbook
Step 1: Write the database playbook

cat > /opt/ansible-playbooks/deploy-database.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Deploy Database Server
  hosts: databases
  become: yes
  vars:
    db_packages:
      - postgresql-server
      - postgresql-contrib
      - python3-psycopg2
    db_name: appdb
    db_user: appuser
    db_password: "{{ vault_db_password | default('ChangeMe123') }}"

  tasks:
    - name: Install PostgreSQL packages
      dnf:
        name: "{{ db_packages }}"
        state: present

    - name: Initialize PostgreSQL database
      command: postgresql-setup --initdb
      args:
        creates: /var/lib/pgsql/data/postgresql.conf

    - name: Start and enable PostgreSQL
      systemd:
        name: postgresql
        state: started
        enabled: yes

    - name: Configure PostgreSQL authentication for local connections
      lineinfile:
        path: /var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf
        regexp: '^local\s+all\s+all\s+peer'
        line: 'local   all             all                                     md5'
      notify: restart postgresql

    - name: Create application database
      postgresql_db:
        name: "{{ db_name }}"
        state: present
      become_user: postgres

    - name: Create application database user
      postgresql_user:
        name: "{{ db_user }}"
        password: "{{ db_password }}"
        db: "{{ db_name }}"
        priv: ALL
        state: present
      become_user: postgres

    - name: Open firewall for PostgreSQL
      firewalld:
        port: 5432/tcp
        permanent: yes
        state: enabled
        immediate: yes
      when: ansible_facts.services['firewalld.service'] is defined

  handlers:
    - name: restart postgresql
      systemd:
        name: postgresql
        state: restarted
EOF

Deliverable: Running ansible-playbook -i /opt/ansible-inventories/production.yml /opt/ansible-playbooks/deploy-database.yml --syntax-check returns no errors.
Subtask 2.3: Configure AWX Project, Inventory, and Job Templates
Step 1: Create a Project in AWX

    Navigate to Resources -> Projects.
    Click Add.
    Fill in:
        Name: Production Deployment
        Organization: Default
        Source Control Type: Manual
        Playbook Directory: /opt/ansible-playbooks (select from the dropdown after the directory is synced into the AWX project base path, or use Git if you push these files to a repository)

Step 2: Create the Inventory and Hosts in AWX

    Navigate to Resources -> Inventories -> Add -> Add Inventory.
    Set Name: Production Infrastructure, Organization: Default.
    Open the inventory, go to the Hosts tab, and add:

web-server-1

ansible_host: 192.168.1.10
ansible_user: ansible
server_role: primary

database-server

ansible_host: 192.168.1.20
ansible_user: ansible
db_type: postgresql

    Go to the Groups tab and create groups webservers and databases, then assign web-server-1 to webservers and database-server to databases.

Step 3: Create the SSH Credential

    Navigate to Resources -> Credentials -> Add.
    Set:
        Name: Ansible SSH Key
        Credential Type: Machine
        Username: ansible
        SSH Private Key: paste the contents of the private key generated in Task 3, Subtask 3.1

Step 4: Create Job Templates

Web Server Deployment Template:

    Navigate to Resources -> Templates -> Add -> Add Job Template.
    Configure:
        Name: Deploy Web Servers
        Job Type: Run
        Inventory: Production Infrastructure
        Project: Production Deployment
        Playbook: deploy-webserver.yml
        Credentials: Ansible SSH Key
        Limit: webservers
        Privilege Escalation: enabled (check the "Privilege Escalation" box)
        Privilege Escalation Method: sudo (ensure the ansible SSH user has passwordless sudo access configured with visudo on the managed nodes, e.g. ansible ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:ALL)

Database Deployment Template:

    Create another job template:
        Name: Deploy Database Server
        Job Type: Run
        Inventory: Production Infrastructure
        Project: Production Deployment
        Playbook: deploy-database.yml
        Credentials: Ansible SSH Key
        Limit: databases
        Privilege Escalation: enabled
        Privilege Escalation Method: sudo (same passwordless sudo requirement as above)

Deliverable: Both job templates appear in the Templates list, and launching Deploy Web Servers completes with status Successful (green checkmark) in the Jobs view.
